.TH CHTTPSVR_REGISTER_HANDLER 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_register_handler \- register a buffered handler on the root router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_register_handler(chttpsvr srv, chttp_method_t method,
                           const char *pattern,
                           chttpsvr_handler_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Registers fn on the root router as the handler for requests that
match method and pattern.  pattern is made of segments separated by a
forward slash, and a segment of the form {name} is a named path
parameter.

A method of CHTTP_ANY registers a wildcard route that matches any of
the seven real HTTP methods (CHTTP_GET, CHTTP_POST, CHTTP_PUT,
CHTTP_DELETE, CHTTP_PATCH, CHTTP_HEAD and CHTTP_OPTIONS) on the given
pattern; the handler can call chttpsvr_req_method to get the method
that really arrived.  Registration order sets the priority: a route
for one method registered before a CHTTP_ANY route on the same
pattern wins for its own method, and the CHTTP_ANY route catches
every other one of the seven.

A route registered for CHTTP_GET also serves HEAD requests for the
same path, as RFC 9110 section 9.3.2 requires.  The handler runs
exactly as it does for GET, and the server sends the headers of the
response, including content-length whenever the handler wrote a body,
without the body.  A route registered explicitly for CHTTP_HEAD or
for CHTTP_ANY on that same path wins over this fallback, in whichever
order you register the two, so a path that must answer HEAD
differently from GET can do so.  A path registered for neither GET
nor HEAD still answers 405 to a HEAD request.

CHTTP_ANY is a placeholder for registration time alone and is never
the method of a request that really arrives.  A request whose method
the server does not recognize at all (a WebDAV verb such as PROPFIND,
a verb such as TRACE or CONNECT, or a custom verb) never reaches any
handler, including one registered with CHTTP_ANY: the server rejects
it with 501 Not Implemented before routing runs, and even before it
parses the path.  See chttpsvr_req_method(3) for the guarantee that
this gives a handler.

pattern must start with '/'; a pattern that does not is rejected at
once with ccol_invalid_args.

The name inside a {name} segment must contain only characters from
[A-Za-z0-9_].  A name with any other character, such as a space, a
hyphen or a dot, is rejected with ccol_invalid_args, because
chttpsvr_req_param could never look it up.  A pattern that uses the
same {name} more than once, for example /a/{id}/b/{id}, is also
rejected with ccol_invalid_args, because nothing could reach the value
that the second one captures: chttpsvr_req_param always returns the
FIRST name that matches.

The values of path segments (literal segments and captured parameters
alike) are decoded by the path rules of RFC 3986: a percent-encoded
sequence such as %20 is decoded, but a '+' stays a literal '+'.  This
matches the decoded path that chttpsvr_req_path returns.  It differs
from the decoding of a query string, which follows
application/x-www-form-urlencoded and reads a '+' as a space; that
convention applies only to chttpsvr_req_query and
chttpsvr_req_query_one, not to a path parameter.

A mounted prefix owns every path under it, and the most specific mount
that owns a path handles the request alone; see
chttpsvr_subrouter(3).  The routes that this function registers sit on
the root router, which handles only paths under no mount, so a
root-level pattern never answers a path under a mount, and never
skips the middleware of that mount, however broad it is and whenever
you registered it.  Registration order plays no part in this.  Within
one router, the server tries the routes in registration order.

The router scans every registered route for a match on the path and
the method, so you can register more than one method for the same
path, and every one of them is reachable, whatever the registration
order.  When at least one route matches the path but none matches the
method, the server answers 405 with an Allow header, as RFC 9110
section 15.5.6 requires, listing every method that a route of the
handling router accepts for the path, in the order GET, HEAD, POST,
PUT, DELETE, PATCH, OPTIONS; HEAD is in the list wherever a CHTTP_GET
route serves it.  When no route matches the path, it answers 404.
For a path under a mount, the owning sub-router answers the 404 or
the 405, and its middleware runs for it.  A request whose method the
server does not recognize at all is rejected even earlier, with 501,
before any route matching runs; see the notes on CHTTP_ANY above.

Path matching also checks the percent-encoded sequences: a request
with an invalid encoding in any path segment does not match the route
and gets a 404, whatever methods you registered for that pattern.
The bad segment can be a literal one, for example /bad%ZZusers/{id}
against /users/{id}, or a captured {name} parameter, for example
/users/bad%ZZvalue against /users/{id}.  A percent-encoded sequence
that decodes to a literal NUL byte (%00) also counts as an invalid
encoding, rather than being decoded and compared as it is.

You can register the same pair of method and pattern more than once,
and each call succeeds and returns ccol_success, but under a
first-one-wins policy the server only ever calls the FIRST handler
that you registered.

A pattern with two consecutive slashes, for example /foo//bar, is
rejected at registration time, and so is one with a trailing slash,
for example /foo/.  Nor does the server treat a trailing slash on a
request path as equivalent: a request for /users/42/ does not match
the pattern /users/{id}, and since a pattern itself must not end
in '/', you cannot register the trailing-slash form as a route of its
own.  If the server must accept such a path, normalize it before it
reaches the server.

The handler runs on a thread of the worker pool of the server once
the whole body has arrived, and chttpsvr_req_body then returns it.  No
thread waits for a slow client in the meantime: a body that stops
arriving parks its connection without a thread, and any free worker
continues it later.  The thread that runs the handler can therefore
differ from the one that began to read the body, and a handler must
not assume thread affinity across requests.  A response that meets a
full socket parks in the same way after the handler returns.  See
"Slow clients" in chttpsvr_start(3).

This function is thread-safe and can be called at any time, even
while another thread runs chttpsvr_destroy on the same server; a call
that races such a destroy returns ccol_invalid_args and touches no
freed memory.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a stale handle that
                  has already been destroyed; when pattern or fn is
                  NULL; when pattern does not start with '/'; when
                  pattern has two consecutive slashes or a trailing
                  slash; when a {name} segment contains a character
                  outside [A-Za-z0-9_]; or when the same {name}
                  appears more than once.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_use (3),
.BR chttpsvr_subrouter (3)
